   "sha": "5f48e89bae5d7ebc80658676f9050169d9f45b9d",
   "node_id": "C_kwDOSVPsWtoAKDVmNDhlODliYWU1ZDdlYmM4MDY1ODY3NmY5MDUwMTY5ZDlmNDViOWQ",
   "commit": {
    "author": {
     "name": "Contributor",
     "email": "dev@example.com",
     "date": "2026-07-31T01:06:59Z"
    },
    "committer": {
     "name": "Contributor",
     "email": "dev@example.com",
     "date": "2026-07-31T01:06:59Z"
    },
    "message": "fix(install): never let an optional coding agent block the install\n\nClaude Code, Codex, and Pi were installed before Free Claude Code, and any\nfailure among them called fail(), which exits. A missing npm, an offline\nmirror, or an unrelated `pi` on PATH therefore aborted the run before the\nproxy -- the thing the script exists to install -- was installed at all.\nReports of \"it tried to install Pi and then nothing worked\" trace to this.\n\nuv and Free Claude Code now install first, and each coding agent runs through\na wrapper that downgrades its failure to a warning: a subshell for sh, a\ntry/catch for PowerShell. The final summary lists whatever was skipped and\nsays plainly that the proxy works without them. Adds --skip-agents /\n-SkipAgents for machines that do not want the third-party CLIs at all.\n\nThe safety property that motivated the original hard failure is kept: an\nexisting incompatible `claude` or `pi` on PATH is still never replaced\nsilently, it just no longer aborts the install.\n\nAlso rewrites the install tutorial: per-platform steps, an explicit warning\nagainst installing in both PowerShell and WSL (two configs, one server), the\nreopen-your-terminal PATH note, and a troubleshooting table covering the\nfailure modes above.",
